To prevent content from scrolling through a header, you typically want to make the header "sticky" or "fixed" so that it remains in place as the user scrolls. Here’s how you can achieve this:

---

### **CSS Solution**
1. **Using `position: sticky`:**
   ```css
   header {
       position: sticky;
       top: 0;
       z-index: 1000; /* Ensures the header stays above other content */
       background: #fff; /* Optional: Set a background colour to cover content behind */
   }
   ```

   - **`top: 0`**: Ensures the header sticks to the top of the viewport.
   - **`z-index`**: Places the header above other elements.

2. **Using `position: fixed`:**
   ```css
   header {
       position: fixed;
       top: 0;
       width: 100%; /* Optional: Ensures it spans the full width */
       z-index: 1000;
       background: #fff; /* Optional: Prevent transparency */
   }
   ```

   - Fixed positioning keeps the header in place, even as the user scrolls.
   - Unlike `sticky`, `fixed` requires manual adjustments if your header height affects the layout.

---

### **HTML Structure Example**
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Sticky Header</title>
    <style>
        body {
            margin: 0;
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
        }
        header {
            position: sticky;
            top: 0;
            background: #333;
            color: white;
            padding: 10px 20px;
            z-index: 1000;
        }
        main {
            padding: 20px;
            background: #f4f4f4;
            height: 2000px; /* Simulates a long page for scrolling */
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <header>
        My Sticky Header
    </header>
    <main>
        Scroll down to see the effect.
    </main>
</body>
</html>
```

---

### **JavaScript Adjustment (Optional)**
If the header requires dynamic changes or behaviour during scroll events:
```javascript
window.addEventListener("scroll", () => {
    const header = document.querySelector("header");
    if (window.scrollY > 50) {
        header.style.backgroundColor = "rgba(51, 51, 51, 0.8)";
    } else {
        header.style.backgroundColor = "#333";
    }
});
```

---

### Troubleshooting Tips
1. **Content Overlapping:** 
   - Add padding or margin to the content below the header to compensate for the header's height.
     ```css
     main {
         padding-top: 60px; /* Adjust to match the header's height */
     }
     ```

2. **Ensure `z-index`:** 
   - Ensure the header has a higher `z-index` than other elements.

