To align two `<div>` elements to the top of a parent `<div>`, you can use **Flexbox** or **CSS positioning**. The Flexbox approach is cleaner and more modern, so here's how to do it with Flexbox:

### ✅ **Option 1: Flexbox (recommended)**
```html
<div class="parent">
  <div class="child1">Div 1</div>
  <div class="child2">Div 2</div>
</div>
```

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  align-items: flex-start; /* Aligns children to the top */
  gap: 1rem; /* Optional: adds spacing between the children */
}
```

This aligns all children of `.parent` to the top of the parent container.

---

### ✅ **Option 2: Positioning (less common for layout)**
```css
.parent {
  position: relative;
}
.child1, .child2 {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
}
.child2 {
  left: 100px; /* adjust as needed */
}
```

This method works but is less flexible and can break more easily with responsive layouts.

